<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · mountain</p><h1 class="enc-title">Black Hills</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Black Hills are a mountain range in South Dakota and Wyoming, sacred to the Lakota. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-fort-laramie-1868">Fort Laramie Treaty of 1868</a> guaranteed them to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-sioux-reservation">Great Sioux Reservation</a>, but after gold was found during Custer's expedition of 1874 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> seized them in 1877. The Lakota have refused a later court-awarded payment.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/black-hills.jpg" alt="Black Hills" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Runner1928 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Needles_Highway_05.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Lakota tradition holds that the people emerged from Wind Cave in the <b>Black Hills</b>, so that their long migration east and back was a journey home.</blockquote><a href="/culture/lakota-people">Lakota people · Origins</a></li><li><blockquote>Pressed by the Ojibwe and drawn by the bison, many moved west in the 18th century, acquired horses, and by around 1776 the Lakota had reached the <b>Black Hills</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-sioux-nation">Great Sioux Nation</a></li></ul></section>
